This week introduced routing concepts, which significantly expanded my understanding of how networks communicate beyond a single subnet.
By configuring routing tables and enabling forwarding, I learned how routers act as intermediaries between networks.
Working with OSPF demonstrated dynamic routing, where routes update automatically when network conditions change.
Using traceroute, I could visualise the actual path packets take.
A key insight was the difference between static and dynamic routing.
I initially found routing tables confusing, especially identifying next-hop addresses.
After repeated practice, I improved my ability to read and interpret routing entries.
This concept is important in real-world networks where dynamic routing ensures reliability and fault tolerance.
